{"result": {"response": {"id": "gen-1787750631-5D7phDQpThFdXnOF4Pwc", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "The IEEE 802.11ax standard, also recognized as Wi-Fi 6, is a significant advancement within the IEEE 802.11 family of wireless networking protocols. Introduced in 2019, Wi-Fi 6 builds upon previous iterations by enhancing efficiency and speed, particularly in environments with a high density of devices. This standard operates across multiple frequency bands, specifically the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands, with a notable extension to the 6 GHz band, which is associated with the Wi-Fi 6E designation.\n\nKey technological innovations introduced with IEEE 802.11ax include Orthogonal Frequency-Division Multiple Access (OFDMA) and BSS Coloring. OFDMA is a technology that allows for more efficient use of available channels by dividing them into smaller sub-channels, enabling multiple devices to transmit data simultaneously. BSS Coloring, on the other hand, helps to mitigate interference in crowded wireless environments by assigning a \"color\" to different networks, allowing devices to more easily identify and ignore traffic from neighboring networks. Additionally, Target Wake Time (TWT) is another feature that improves efficiency by allowing devices to schedule their wake times, reducing power consumption, especially for battery-powered devices.\n\nThe theoretical maximum speeds for IEEE 802.11ax can reach up to 9.6 Gbps, offering a substantial improvement over previous Wi-Fi standards. While Wi-Fi 6 encompasses the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands, Wi-Fi 6E specifically leverages the newly available 6 GHz spectrum, offering even more capacity and reduced interference due to its pristine nature. In essence, IEEE 802.11ax, or Wi-Fi 6/6E, represents a leap forward in wireless networking, prioritizing greater efficiency, lower latency, and increased throughput, making it ideally suited for the demands of modern, connected environments.", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null, "reasoning": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {"native_finish_reason": "STOP"}}], "created": 1787750631, "model": "google/gemini-2.5-flash-lite", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 397, "prompt_tokens": 338, "total_tokens": 735, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": null, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 0, "rejected_prediction_tokens": null, "image_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 0, "cached_tokens": 0, "video_tokens": 0}, "cost": 0.0001926, "is_byok": false, "cost_details": {"upstream_inference_cost": 0.0001926, "upstream_inference_prompt_cost": 3.38e-05, "upstream_inference_completions_cost": 0.0001588}}, "formatted_response": null, "provider": "Google", "content": "The IEEE 802.11ax standard, also recognized as Wi-Fi 6, is a significant advancement within the IEEE 802.11 family of wireless networking protocols. Introduced in 2019, Wi-Fi 6 builds upon previous iterations by enhancing efficiency and speed, particularly in environments with a high density of devices. This standard operates across multiple frequency bands, specifically the 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz bands, with a notable extension to the 6 GHz band, which is associated with the Wi-Fi 6E designation.\n\nKey technological innovations introduced with IEEE 802.11ax include Orthogonal Frequency-Division Multiple Access (OFDMA) and BSS Coloring. OFDMA is a technology that allows for more efficient use of available channels by dividing them into smaller sub-channels, enabling multiple devices to transmit data simultaneously. BSS Coloring, on the other hand, helps to mitigate interference in crowded wireless environments by assigning a \"color\" to different networks, allow